                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This game looked pretty interesting, so when a friend gifted this to me, I was pretty interested in checking it out. Redium definitely has that cool, pixel noire detective movie kind of feel to it. Sadly, there are abundant issues that prevent this game from seeing its potential. For one, this game features some of the worst translations in any game I've ever played. They are just really bad and largely nonsensical, forcing you to rely on your experience in the game to sort of try to figure out what was actually attempting to be conveyed by the seemingly ranndom jumble of words that appear all too often.\n\nThen there is the issue of pacing: Redium starts out almost insanely slow, going back and forth between various objectives and your apartment to sleep. Then, after a key story moment, your apartment complex is invaded by bad guys. At this point, the game then changes to memorization almost exclusively. Basically, as you progress, you need to remember enemy position as well as where items drop so you can equip said items (weapons) and fire them at baddies before they kill you. These are mostly one to two second sequences, you need need to be quick. Additionally, each time you mess up, you get booted back to the beginning of the sequence. Finally, I got tired of that and so left with the intention of playing again in a day or two. That would be today and every time I click continue, it flashes for a moment and then goes back to the menu. Until/unless that is fixed, there is no literal way to progress without completely starting over, whic I have no intention of doing. However, as I mentioned, there is a neat game underneath all that mess. Hopefully the dev sorts out these issues at some point.\n\n[code]If you found this review helpful and would be interested in supporting my Curator group, it would be appreciated. Cheers.\nhttps://store.steampowered.com/curator/32549618/[/code]",
